{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>On the eighth day, a festival of assembly, one bull, one ram.</b> To show that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not need for His own sake, but only to show the honor of Israel. And it would have been fitting for the festival of assembly to be fifty days after Sukkot, just as Shavuot is fifty days after Pesach. But after Pesach the summer days come and there is no exertion to go up on pilgrimage; whereas after Sukkot the rainy days come and there would be exertion to go up — therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, fixed it immediately after Sukkot. A parable: to a king who marries two wives, one from a nearby place and one from a distant place. To the one who is from a nearby place he says: Now let us make the engagement, and after three months I will return here and we will make the wedding, for the place is close to me. And to the one who is from a distant place he says: Let us make now the engagement and the wedding together, for the place is far and I cannot exert myself to come back here another time. And this is what the liturgical poet composed: 'On the eighth day of the festival of assembly, He determined to gather them at seven, because of the exertion of the woman who gives birth to seven' — and Israel are called thus, as it is said: 'The woman who gives birth to seven languishes,' because she gave birth to seven kings for Gehenna."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:10:22.457725841Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}